ate.It also means that we don't need to use any markers when scanning.\"\n\"As I am trying to get better with my 3D design work. By adding a 3D scanner, it provides a fantastic starting off point with incredibly detailed models of tools and accessories from around the shop!\"\nLearn More >\nWhat We’re Up To\nView all\nEvents\nRevopoint Showcases Advanced 3D Scanning Solutions at Formnext Asia Shenzhen 2026\nFrom August 26 to 28, Formnext Asia Shenzhen 2026 brought together professionals from across the 3D printing,...\nAug 28, 2026\nProgram\nRevopoint Launches New Education Program to Support 3D Scanning in Learning and Research\nRevopoint is launching a new Education Program to make professional 3D scanning more accessible to students, educato...\nAug 25, 2026\n3D Info\nUpgrade Your STEM Gear: Save Up to 20% on Selected 3D Scanners\nRevopoint’s STEM Promotion runs from August 12 to August 31, 2026, bringing limited-time discounts on selected 3D sca...\nAug 14, 2026\nBlog\nView all\nApplications\nFrom a Porsche Chassis to a Carbon-Fiber Body Kit: 3D Scanning Case Study\nLearn how 3D scanning helped PrintedForFun redesign a Porsche body kit, verify fitment, and move from scan to CAD, 3D...\nSep 2, 2026\nBuying Guides\nRevopoint 3D Scanners by Workflow: Which Model Fits Your Project?\nChoosing a 3D scanner is easier when the workflow comes first. A metrology-led mechanical inspection, a scan around a...\nSep 2, 2026\nBuying Guides\nHow to Choose a Professional 3D Scanner for High-Accuracy Projects\nWhen scan data is slightly off, the real delay rarely starts during scanning. It usually appears later, when CAD need...\nAug 26, 2026\nFAQ\nWhat is a Revopoint affordable 3D scanner for beginners and 3D printing?\nIf you’re looking for an affordable Revopoint scanner for beginners and 3D printing, POP 4 is a strong choice for beginners, makers, and 3D printing workflows. Its hybrid blue-laser and infrared scanning modes support small-to-medium objects and detailed surfaces across different scanning conditions. POP 4 also includes AI-powered object segmentation to streamline post-processing and supports 3D Gaussian Splatting for creating photorealistic 3D models.\nIf keeping the budget as low as possible is the priority, INSPIRE 2 is an entry-level alternative with infrared laser and structured-light scanning and full-color capture. For the smallest parts and higher-precision detail capture, MINI 2 is a higher-tier option designed for small objects.\nWhat is a high precision 3D scanner?\nA high precision 3D scanner is designed to capture extremely accurate measurements of objects, often with accuracy down to fractions of a millimeter. These scanners are commonly used in reverse engineering, quality inspection, and product development, where fine details and dimensional accuracy are critical. High precision 3D scanners typically use technologies such as structured light or laser scanning to ensure consistent and reliable results.\nWhat makes a good portable 3D scanner?\nA good portable 3D scanner combines accuracy, fast scanning speed, and ease of use in a compact design. Key factors include lightweight hardware, stable tracking, and the ability to scan on the go without sacrificing detail. Portable 3D scanners are ideal for fieldwork and mobile applications where flexibility and quick setup are essential.\nWhat is a professional 3D scanner?\nA professional 3D scanner is built for commercial and industrial applications, offering higher accuracy, advanced scanning modes, and reliable software support. These scanners are commonly used by engineers, designers, and manufacturers for tasks such as inspection, reverse engineering, and product design. Professional 3D scanners prioritize precision, repeatability, and workflow efficiency.\nWhat is a handheld laser scanner?\nA handheld laser scanner is a portable device that uses laser technology to capture 3D data by projecting laser lines onto an object. It allows users to freely move around the object while scanning, making it suitable for complex shapes and hard to reach areas. Handheld laser scanners are widely used in industrial measurement, automotive applications, and large scale scanning projects.\nWhat is a 3D scanner for printer?\nA 3D scanner for printer is used to digitize physical objects and convert them into 3D models that can be edited and prepared for 3D printing. These scanners help users replicate parts, customize designs, and create printable files with accurate dimensions. They are commonly used in prototyping, repair, and hobbyist 3D printing workflows.\nWhat is a 3D imaging scanner?\nA 3D imaging scanner captures the shape and surface details of objects to create digital 3D models. The scanner itself focuses on acquiring accurate geometry and visual data, while dimensional measurements and analysis are typically completed with dedicated measurement or inspection software. With the support of suitable software, 3D imaging scanners are widely used for design visualization, digital archiving, and inspection workflows across various industries.\nOffers\nTrade-in\nRecycle Your Scanner for a Discounted New One\nLearn More >\nBulk Purchase Program\nMaximize Your Bulk Purchase Savings\nLearn More >\nSpecial Discount Program\nExtra 5% Off for Educators, Students, Doctors, and Veterans\nLearn More >\nAwards\nRed Dot Best of the Best 2024\nIDEA Award Finalist 2023\nThe Best 3D Scanner in 2023\nThe Best 3D Scanner in 2023\nTOP 10 Office Accessories\nBest 3D Scanners\nLearn About Us >\nWhy Buy Directly From Revopoint?\n30-Day\nNo-hassle Returns\n30-Day\nFree Exchange\n2-Year\nWarranty\n24/7\nCustomer Service\nFree Shipping\nfor Most 3D Scanners\n24/7 Online Support\nSpeak to a Revopoint expert now. Whether by phone, chat, or email, we’re ready to help you with all of your product questions and issues.\nLearn More >\nFollow Us\nFollow us on social media for the latest updates, fun contests, giveaways, offline events, and spectacular user showcase